  prose(<<~'CANONICAL_PROSE', origin: :published)
    ## Principle
    
    A Nightshelf technique for cloaking fire-light beneath layers of crystallized aurora-ice, rendering flames invisible to the naked eye while preserving their thermal output. The mechanism is paradoxical: fire burns *within* ice rather than against it, producing heat that casts no glow and leaves no shadow. Proximity is detectable only by frost-burn on exposed tissue.
    
    ## Origin and Development
    
    Veil☽ Glow originated in the shadow labs of ☽'scar Ramtatus~, a Nightshelf volcanic complex, during the Orca Incursion. The ☽'shadow Covens' Order, a Nightshelf esoteric faction, refined it from the crude thermal-containment principles of The Shell Hatch — a process conducted in deep-ice laboratories whose ice-memory impressions I will describe only as distressing.
    
    Crypt-Chancellor Silvcaeclerae-fawn~, founder and governor of the deep colony ☽'slew Nisaemesius~, mastered the technique sufficiently to weaponize it. Under her direction, invisible braziers were deployed to orchestrate ambushes against orca war-pods. Historians now argue Veil☽ Glow tipped the scales of engagements that were otherwise unwinnable.
    
    Its practitioners span an unlikely range: the Collective☽ Of Led∴merch, E'frostlings, The Midnight Claws, The Wake-Singers, and Depth-Purveyor ☽'Cilocum among them. A technique born in one faction's laboratories does not remain proprietary for long.
    
    ## Infrastructure Applications
    
    Beyond concealment, Veil☽ Glow was adapted as cooling infrastructure in ☽'slew Nisaemesius~'s deep tunnels, where crystallized aurora threads regulated volcanic temperatures along mining routes. Surface colonies deployed similar arrays to stabilize inhabited sections, including installations near the Promenade Ward.
    
    ## Costs and Failures
    
    The physiological toll on practitioners accumulates in stages: deterioration of night-sight from prolonged aurora-light exposure, a persistent flipper tremor, and — per multiple accounts — dreams of internal burning. No systematic study of long-term effects was conducted before the technique proliferated.
    
    The infrastructure failures proved equally consequential. During the Abys∴uina, the catastrophic steam eruption beneath ☽'slew Nisaemesius~, the cooling threads snapped, removing a critical safeguard against trapped volcanic heat. During the Collapse, Promenade Ward arrays shattered in cascading failure, leaving whole sections fatally cold. The subsequent Aurora-touched succession crisis saw Veil☽ Glow infrastructure freeze fire-core shipments between colonies, severing supply lines.
    
    ☽'Netemia Glow-bringer, a Nightshelf figure of some renown, reportedly refused to employ Veil☽ Glow even when orcas besieged her colony. Some accounts hold that refusal as the reason the colony fell. The ice-memory at the relevant stratum is inconclusive.
  CANONICAL_PROSE
  annotation "note_1771588381503_0", anchor: "The Wake-Singers", text: "Reader, sit with this list for a moment. A technique engineered to hide fire from orca raiders — specifically, to render Nightshelf positions invisible during the Incursion — is now practiced by an orca blood-cult whose stated objective is waking a dead god through accumulated sacrifice. The Wake-Singers did not steal Veil☽ Glow. They learned it through the same channels everyone else did: proliferation along trade routes the Nightshelf could not or would not control. The weapon designed to win the war walked itself into the enemy's theology. No one measured that cost either.", type: :commentary, display: :full
  annotation "note_1771588381503_1", anchor: "engagements that were otherwise unwinnable", text: "\"Otherwise unwinnable\" assumes the engagements were defensive. At least two of the actions conventionally attributed to orca aggression during the Incursion were retaliatory strikes provoked by Nightshelf encroachment on hunting grounds. The ice at those strata is unambiguous. Whether a technique \"tips the scales\" reads differently when the scales were tipped by the defenders first.", type: :skepticism, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771588381503_2", anchor: "I will describe only as distressing", text: "I hedged. The ice-memory in those laboratories preserves not the experiments but the failures — penguins whose thermal signatures inverted. Heat flowing inward. The impressions carry a sensation I have felt nowhere else in the archive. I chose 'distressing' because the main text required a word the reader could move past. This margin does not.", type: :commentary, display: :popout
